(function(m,c){var L=window.ACADEMY.lessons;for(var id in c){Object.assign(L[id],c[id]);}(window.ACADEMY_LOADED=window.ACADEMY_LOADED||{})[m]=1;})("helm",{"helm/what-helm-is-and-why-use-it":{"html":"\u003cp>A real application on Kubernetes is not one YAML file. It is a Deployment, a Service, an Ingress, a ConfigMap, a ServiceAccount, maybe a HorizontalPodAutoscaler, and the values differ between development and production. Copying and editing those files for each environment leads to drift and mistakes. Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes: it bundles the files as a chart, fills in the differences from values, and tracks every install as a release you can upgrade or roll back.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The problem Helm solves\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Charts, releases, repositories and values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Install your first chart and look at what it created\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>What Helm does not do\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"the-problem\">The problem\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Plain manifests have three weaknesses. They repeat themselves (the same label in ten places), they cannot easily vary by environment (replica count, image tag, hostname), and they have no concept of &quot;this set of objects is one application at version 1.4&quot;. Tools such as Kustomize patch plain YAML; Helm adds \u003cstrong>templating\u003c/strong> and \u003cstrong>packaging\u003c/strong>. With Helm, one chart describes the application, and a small values file describes how this installation differs. The same chart can be installed ten times in ten namespaces.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"four-words-to-know\">Four words to know\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>chart\u003c/strong> is a package: a folder (or a \u003ccode>.tgz\u003c/code> file) with templates and default values.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>release\u003c/strong> is one installation of a chart in a cluster, with a name, such as \u003ccode>shop-web\u003c/code>. Install the same chart twice and you get two releases.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>A \u003cstrong>repository\u003c/strong> is a place that serves charts, either an HTTP index or an OCI registry.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Values\u003c/strong> are the settings that fill the templates. Defaults come from the chart; you override them.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Helm 3 has no server component. The \u003ccode>helm\u003c/code> command talks to the Kubernetes API with your kubeconfig and stores the release history as Secrets in the release namespace.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"install-something\">Install something\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Use a made-up repository name; the commands are the same for any repository.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm repo add acme https://charts.example.com\nhelm repo update\nhelm search repo acme/web\nhelm install shop-web acme/web --namespace shop --create-namespace\nhelm list --namespace shop\nhelm status shop-web --namespace shop\nkubectl get all --namespace shop\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm repo add acme https://charts.example.com\nhelm repo update\nhelm search repo acme/web\nhelm install shop-web acme/web --namespace shop --create-namespace\nhelm list --namespace shop\nhelm status shop-web --namespace shop\nkubectl get all --namespace shop\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>helm install NAME CHART\u003c/code> renders the templates, sends the resulting manifests to the cluster and records revision 1. \u003ccode>helm list\u003c/code> shows releases, \u003ccode>helm status\u003c/code> shows the state and the notes the chart prints, and \u003ccode>kubectl\u003c/code> shows the real objects. Everything Helm created carries labels such as \u003ccode>app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm\u003c/code> and an annotation with the release name.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-helm-does-not-do\">What Helm does not do\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Helm does not make bad YAML good, does not manage secrets by itself, and does not watch the cluster afterwards: it applies changes when you run a command. If someone edits an object with \u003ccode>kubectl edit\u003c/code>, Helm will not notice until the next upgrade, which may overwrite the edit. Many teams run Helm from a pipeline or a controller that applies the chart on every change from version control, so the cluster always matches what was reviewed. Charts from the internet are code that runs in your cluster: read them, pin versions and prefer sources you trust.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You install the same chart twice with different release names in one namespace. What do you have?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Two releases of the same chart, each with its own objects and history (if the names do not clash).\u003c/p>\u003cp>A release is an installation, a chart is the package.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-three-copies-of-the-same-yaml\">Incident: Three copies of the same YAML\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Staging and production drifted apart because each had its own set of copied manifests.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"diff -r k8s/staging k8s/prod | wc -l\ngrep -rn &quot;replicas:&quot; k8s/prod/deployment.yaml k8s/staging/deployment.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>diff -r k8s/staging k8s/prod | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wc\u003c/span> -l\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">143\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>grep -rn \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;replicas:&quot;\u003c/span> k8s/prod/deployment.yaml k8s/staging/deployment.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">k8s/prod/deployment.yaml:8:  replicas: 6\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">k8s/staging/deployment.yaml:8:  replicas: 2\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Over months, people fixed one environment and forgot the other, so staging no longer tested what production ran. The team moved to a single chart with values-staging.yaml and values-prod.yaml. The diff between environments shrank to a short values file, and a fix to a template reaches both at once.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Copying manifests per environment\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Installing charts from unknown sources without reading them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Editing live objects with kubectl and expecting Helm to know\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using latest as a chart or image version\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"the-problem","text":"The problem"},{"id":"four-words-to-know","text":"Four words to know"},{"id":"install-something","text":"Install something"},{"id":"what-helm-does-not-do","text":"What Helm does not do"},{"id":"incident-three-copies-of-the-same-yaml","text":"Incident: Three copies of the same YAML"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm: Introduction","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/intro/using_helm/"},{"t":"Helm: Quickstart","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/intro/quickstart/"}],"text":"A real application on Kubernetes is not one YAML file. It is a Deployment, a Service, an Ingress, a ConfigMap, a ServiceAccount, maybe a HorizontalPodAutoscaler, and the values differ between development and production. Copying and editing those files for each environment leads to drift and mistakes. Helm is the package manager for Kubernetes: it bundles the files as a chart, fills in the differences from values, and tracks every install as a release you can upgrade or roll back. What you will learn The problem Helm solves Charts, releases, repositories and values Install your first chart and look at what it created What Helm does not do The problem Plain manifests have three weaknesses. They repeat themselves (the same label in ten places), they cannot easily vary by environment (replica count, image tag, hostname), and they have no concept of \"this set of objects is one application at version 1.4\". Tools such as Kustomize patch plain YAML; Helm adds templating and packaging . With Helm, one chart describes the application, and a small values file describes how this installation differs. The same chart can be installed ten times in ten namespaces. Four words to know A chart is a package: a folder (or a .tgz file) with templates and default values. A release is one installation of a chart in a cluster, with a name, such as shop-web . Install the same chart twice and you get two releases. A repository is a place that serves charts, either an HTTP index or an OCI registry. Values are the settings that fill the templates. Defaults come from the chart; you override them. Helm 3 has no server component. The helm command talks to the Kubernetes API with your kubeconfig and stores the release history as Secrets in the release namespace. Install something Use a made-up repository name; the commands are the same for any repository. helm repo add acme https://charts.example.com helm repo update helm search repo acme/web helm install shop-web acme/web --namespace shop --create-namespace helm list --namespace shop helm status shop-web --namespace shop kubectl get all --namespace shop helm install NAME CHART renders the templates, sends the resulting manifests to the cluster and records revision 1. helm list shows releases, helm status shows the state and the notes the chart prints, and kubectl shows the real objects. Everything Helm created carries labels such as app.kubernetes.io/managed-by: Helm and an annotation with the release name. What Helm does not do Helm does not make bad YAML good, does not manage secrets by itself, and does not watch the cluster afterwards: it applies changes when you run a command. If someone edits an object with kubectl edit , Helm will not notice until the next upgrade, which may overwrite the edit. Many teams run Helm from a pipeline or a controller that applies the chart on every change from version control, so the cluster always matches what was reviewed. Charts from the internet are code that runs in your cluster: read them, pin versions and prefer sources you trust. Predict first. You install the same chart twice with different release names in one namespace. What do you have? Your guess Answer: Two releases of the same chart, each with its own objects and history (if the names do not clash). A release is an installation, a chart is the package. Incident: Three copies of the same YAML Incident Staging and production drifted apart because each had its own set of copied manifests. $ diff -r k8s/staging k8s/prod | wc -l 143 $ grep -rn \"replicas:\" k8s/prod/deployment.yaml k8s/staging/deployment.yaml k8s/prod/deployment.yaml:8: replicas: 6 k8s/staging/deployment.yaml:8: replicas: 2 Over months, people fixed one environment and forgot the other, so staging no longer tested what production ran. The team moved to a single chart with values-staging.yaml and values-prod.yaml. The diff between environments shrank to a short values file, and a fix to a template reaches both at once. Common mistakes Copying manifes"},"helm/installing-charts-and-values":{"html":"\u003cp>Most of your first week with Helm is spent answering one question: how do I change this setting? Every chart exposes its options as values. Knowing how to read them, override them and preview the result before it touches the cluster is the core skill, and it also protects you from the most common mistake: installing with defaults that are not meant for production.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Search repositories and read a chart before installing\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Inspect default values with helm show values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Override values with -f files and --set, and know the precedence\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Preview with helm template and --dry-run\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"look-before-you-install\">Look before you install\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm repo add acme https://charts.example.com\nhelm repo update\nhelm search repo acme --versions | head\nhelm show chart acme/web --version 2.4.1\nhelm show readme acme/web --version 2.4.1\nhelm show values acme/web --version 2.4.1 &gt; defaults.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm repo add acme https://charts.example.com\nhelm repo update\nhelm search repo acme --versions | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span>\nhelm show chart acme/web --version 2.4.1\nhelm show readme acme/web --version 2.4.1\nhelm show values acme/web --version 2.4.1 &gt; defaults.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Always pin \u003ccode>--version\u003c/code>, so a repository update does not change what you install. Read the readme and the defaults: look for the image tag, replica count, resource requests, service type, ingress settings and any &quot;persistence&quot; or &quot;auth&quot; section. A chart that enables a persistent volume or a public LoadBalancer by default may not be what you want.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"override-with-a-values-file\">Override with a values file\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Write only the keys you change, in a file under version control:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# values-prod.yaml\nreplicaCount: 4\nimage:\n  repository: registry.example.com/shop/web\n  tag: &quot;1.8.2&quot;\ningress:\n  enabled: true\n  host: shop.example.com\nresources:\n  requests:\n    cpu: 250m\n    memory: 256Mi\n  limits:\n    memory: 512Mi\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicaCount:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">4\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repository:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tag:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1.8.2&quot;\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">ingress:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enabled:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">host:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop.example.com\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">requests:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">cpu:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">250m\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">256Mi\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">limits:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">memory:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">512Mi\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm install shop-web acme/web --version 2.4.1 -n shop -f values-prod.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm install shop-web acme/web --version 2.4.1 -n shop -f values-prod.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Maps are merged key by key, but \u003cstrong>lists are replaced whole\u003c/strong>, so overriding one item of a list means supplying the entire list. Quote version-like strings (\u003ccode>&quot;1.8.2&quot;\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>&quot;true&quot;\u003c/code>) so YAML does not turn them into numbers or booleans.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"set-and-precedence\">--set and precedence\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>For a one-off change use \u003ccode>--set\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml --set image.tag=1.8.3\nhelm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop --set-string podAnnotations.build=2026\nhelm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop --set &quot;ingress.hosts[0]=shop.example.com&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> image.tag=1.8.3\nhelm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop --set-string podAnnotations.build=2026\nhelm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;ingress.hosts[0]=shop.example.com&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>From lowest to highest priority: the chart&#39;s \u003ccode>values.yaml\u003c/code>, parent chart values, files given with \u003ccode>-f\u003c/code> (later files win), then \u003ccode>--set\u003c/code>. Pipelines usually use files for everything stable and \u003ccode>--set image.tag=...\u003c/code> for the one value that changes on every release. Avoid long \u003ccode>--set\u003c/code> lists; they are hard to review and reproduce.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"preview-and-verify\">Preview and verify\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm template shop-web acme/web --version 2.4.1 -f values-prod.yaml &gt; rendered.yaml\nhelm install shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml --dry-run --debug\nhelm get values shop-web -n shop -a        # all values in use\nhelm get manifest shop-web -n shop         # the YAML that was applied\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm template shop-web acme/web --version 2.4.1 -f values-prod.yaml &gt; rendered.yaml\nhelm install shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml --dry-run --debug\nhelm get values shop-web -n shop -a        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># all values in use\u003c/span>\nhelm get manifest shop-web -n shop         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># the YAML that was applied\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> renders locally without a cluster, so you can lint it, run it through policy checks and review the diff in a pull request. After installing, \u003ccode>helm get values -a\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>helm get manifest\u003c/code> show what is really deployed. Review the rendered output for the things that matter: image tags, resource limits, security context and exposed services.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You set `ingress.hosts[0]=a.example.com` with --set, but the chart default list had two hosts. What happens to the second?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Lists are replaced, not merged, so you must give the whole list you want; the second default host is not kept.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Values merge by map key, but lists replace.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-upgrade-that-reset-the-settings\">Incident: The upgrade that reset the settings\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>After a routine image bump, the production site lost its custom hostname and resource limits.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop --set image.tag=1.8.3\nhelm get values shop-web -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> image.tag=1.8.3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Release &quot;shop-web&quot; has been upgraded. Happy Helming!\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm get values shop-web -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">USER-SUPPLIED VALUES:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">image:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  tag: 1.8.3\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The engineer ran an upgrade with only --set and no values file, so Helm used the chart defaults for everything else: the earlier overrides were not carried over (--reuse-values would have kept them, with its own risks). The fix was a rule that every upgrade passes the same -f files plus the one changed value, stored in the repository, and a pipeline step that runs helm diff before applying.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Installing with unread defaults\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not pinning chart versions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Upgrading with only --set and losing earlier overrides\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Using floating tags such as latest in values\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"look-before-you-install","text":"Look before you install"},{"id":"override-with-a-values-file","text":"Override with a values file"},{"id":"set-and-precedence","text":"--set and precedence"},{"id":"preview-and-verify","text":"Preview and verify"},{"id":"incident-the-upgrade-that-reset-the-settings","text":"Incident: The upgrade that reset the settings"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm: Values Files","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/chart_template_guide/values_files/"},{"t":"helm install","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/helm/helm_install/"}],"text":"Most of your first week with Helm is spent answering one question: how do I change this setting? Every chart exposes its options as values. Knowing how to read them, override them and preview the result before it touches the cluster is the core skill, and it also protects you from the most common mistake: installing with defaults that are not meant for production. What you will learn Search repositories and read a chart before installing Inspect default values with helm show values Override values with -f files and --set, and know the precedence Preview with helm template and --dry-run Look before you install helm repo add acme https://charts.example.com helm repo update helm search repo acme --versions | head helm show chart acme/web --version 2.4.1 helm show readme acme/web --version 2.4.1 helm show values acme/web --version 2.4.1 > defaults.yaml Always pin --version , so a repository update does not change what you install. Read the readme and the defaults: look for the image tag, replica count, resource requests, service type, ingress settings and any \"persistence\" or \"auth\" section. A chart that enables a persistent volume or a public LoadBalancer by default may not be what you want. Override with a values file Write only the keys you change, in a file under version control: # values-prod.yaml replicaCount: 4 image: repository: registry.example.com/shop/web tag: \"1.8.2\" ingress: enabled: true host: shop.example.com resources: requests: cpu: 250m memory: 256Mi limits: memory: 512Mi helm install shop-web acme/web --version 2.4.1 -n shop -f values-prod.yaml Maps are merged key by key, but lists are replaced whole , so overriding one item of a list means supplying the entire list. Quote version-like strings ( \"1.8.2\" , \"true\" ) so YAML does not turn them into numbers or booleans. --set and precedence For a one-off change use --set : helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml -- set image.tag=1.8.3 helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop --set-string podAnnotations.build=2026 helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop -- set \"ingress.hosts[0]=shop.example.com\" From lowest to highest priority: the chart&#39;s values.yaml , parent chart values, files given with -f (later files win), then --set . Pipelines usually use files for everything stable and --set image.tag=... for the one value that changes on every release. Avoid long --set lists; they are hard to review and reproduce. Preview and verify helm template shop-web acme/web --version 2.4.1 -f values-prod.yaml > rendered.yaml helm install shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml --dry-run --debug helm get values shop-web -n shop -a # all values in use helm get manifest shop-web -n shop # the YAML that was applied helm template renders locally without a cluster, so you can lint it, run it through policy checks and review the diff in a pull request. After installing, helm get values -a and helm get manifest show what is really deployed. Review the rendered output for the things that matter: image tags, resource limits, security context and exposed services. Predict first. You set `ingress.hosts[0]=a.example.com` with --set, but the chart default list had two hosts. What happens to the second? Your guess Answer: Lists are replaced, not merged, so you must give the whole list you want; the second default host is not kept. Values merge by map key, but lists replace. Incident: The upgrade that reset the settings Incident After a routine image bump, the production site lost its custom hostname and resource limits. $ helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop -- set image.tag=1.8.3 Release \"shop-web\" has been upgraded. Happy Helming! $ helm get values shop-web -n shop USER-SUPPLIED VALUES: image: tag: 1.8.3 The engineer ran an upgrade with only --set and no values file, so Helm used the chart defaults for everything else: the earlier overrides were not carried over (--reuse-values would have kept them, with its own risks). The fix was a rule that every upgrade passes the "},"helm/anatomy-of-a-chart":{"html":"\u003cp>To use Helm well you will soon need to read a chart or write a small one for your own application. A chart is just a directory with a fixed layout, so it is easier than it looks. Understanding what each file is for lets you read third-party charts quickly and write your own without cargo-culting the generated example.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>The files of a chart and their purpose\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Chart.yaml: version and appVersion\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>values.yaml as the chart interface\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>_helpers.tpl, NOTES.txt and helm lint\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"generate-and-look\">Generate and look\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm create shop-web\ntree shop-web\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm create shop-web\ntree shop-web\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"shop-web/\n  Chart.yaml          name, version, appVersion, description, dependencies\n  values.yaml         default values: the interface of the chart\n  charts/             packaged subcharts (dependencies)\n  templates/\n    deployment.yaml\n    service.yaml\n    ingress.yaml\n    hpa.yaml\n    serviceaccount.yaml\n    _helpers.tpl      named template snippets, not rendered on their own\n    NOTES.txt         message printed after install\n    tests/test-connection.yaml\n  .helmignore         files to leave out of the package\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">shop-web/\n  Chart.yaml          name, version, appVersion, description, dependencies\n  values.yaml         default values: the interface of the chart\n  charts/             packaged subcharts (dependencies)\n  templates/\n    deployment.yaml\n    service.yaml\n    ingress.yaml\n    hpa.yaml\n    serviceaccount.yaml\n    _helpers.tpl      named template snippets, not rendered on their own\n    NOTES.txt         message printed after install\n    tests/test-connection.yaml\n  .helmignore         files to leave out of the package\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The generated chart is a good starting point but includes things you may not need. Delete what you do not use (the hpa, the test, the ingress) and keep the chart small.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"chart-yaml\">Chart.yaml\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: v2\nname: shop-web\ndescription: The shop web frontend\ntype: application\nversion: 0.3.1        # version of the chart (packaging)\nappVersion: &quot;1.8.2&quot;   # version of the application inside\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">apiVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">v2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-web\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">description:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">The\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">web\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">frontend\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">application\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">0.3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">.1\u003c/span>        \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># version of the chart (packaging)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">appVersion:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;1.8.2&quot;\u003c/span>   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># version of the application inside\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>version\u003c/code> follows semantic versioning and must increase whenever the chart changes, because repositories and OCI registries treat versions as immutable. \u003ccode>appVersion\u003c/code> is informational: the version of the app (often the default image tag). Do not confuse them: you may release chart 0.3.2 with the same app, or app 1.9.0 with chart 0.4.0.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"values-yaml-is-the-interface\">values.yaml is the interface\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>The values file documents every setting a user may change, with safe defaults and comments:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"replicaCount: 2\nimage:\n  repository: registry.example.com/shop/web\n  tag: &quot;&quot;            # empty means use appVersion\n  pullPolicy: IfNotPresent\nservice:\n  type: ClusterIP\n  port: 80\nresources: {}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">replicaCount:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">2\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">image:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repository:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">registry.example.com/shop/web\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">tag:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;&quot;\u003c/span>            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># empty means use appVersion\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">pullPolicy:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">IfNotPresent\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">service:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">type:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">ClusterIP\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">port:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">80\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">resources:\u003c/span> {}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Treat it like an API: keep names stable, group related keys, and avoid exposing every Kubernetes field. Fewer, well named values make a chart easier to use. Add \u003ccode>values.schema.json\u003c/code> to validate input, so a typo such as \u003ccode>replicaCont\u003c/code> fails with a message instead of being ignored.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"helpers-notes-and-linting\">Helpers, notes and linting\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>_helpers.tpl\u003c/code> defines reusable snippets with \u003ccode>define\u003c/code>, such as the standard name and labels, used with \u003ccode>include\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>NOTES.txt\u003c/code> is a template printed after install; use it for how to reach the app. Check your work often:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm lint shop-web\nhelm template shop-web ./shop-web | kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f -\nhelm package shop-web          # creates shop-web-0.3.1.tgz\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm lint shop-web\nhelm template shop-web ./shop-web | kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f -\nhelm package shop-web          \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># creates shop-web-0.3.1.tgz\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>helm lint\u003c/code> finds missing fields and bad syntax; rendering and applying with a dry run catches Kubernetes schema errors. Run both in CI for every chart change.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> You change a template but forget to raise Chart.yaml version and push the package to the repository. What goes wrong?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> Users who already have that version will not get the change, or the push is rejected, because chart versions are meant to be immutable.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Every published change needs a new chart version.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-same-version-different-chart\">Incident: Same version, different chart\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>Two clusters installed chart 1.2.0 a month apart and behaved differently.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm get manifest shop-web -n shop | grep -c &quot;readinessProbe&quot;\nhelm pull acme/web --version 1.2.0 --untar &amp;&amp; grep -c readinessProbe web/templates/deployment.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm get manifest shop-web -n shop | grep -c \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;readinessProbe&quot;\u003c/span>\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">0\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm pull acme/web --version 1.2.0 --untar &amp;&amp; grep -c readinessProbe web/templates/deployment.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">1\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Someone had republished a different package under the same chart version, so the clusters held different content. The repository now refuses to overwrite an existing version, the pipeline bumps the chart version automatically from the change type, and packages are signed or stored in an OCI registry with immutable tags.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Reusing the same chart version for changed content\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Exposing every Kubernetes field as a value\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Leaving unused generated templates in the chart\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Skipping helm lint in CI\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"generate-and-look","text":"Generate and look"},{"id":"chart-yaml","text":"Chart.yaml"},{"id":"values-yaml-is-the-interface","text":"values.yaml is the interface"},{"id":"helpers-notes-and-linting","text":"Helpers, notes and linting"},{"id":"incident-same-version-different-chart","text":"Incident: Same version, different chart"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm: Charts","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts/"},{"t":"Helm: Chart Best Practices","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/chart_best_practices/"}],"text":"To use Helm well you will soon need to read a chart or write a small one for your own application. A chart is just a directory with a fixed layout, so it is easier than it looks. Understanding what each file is for lets you read third-party charts quickly and write your own without cargo-culting the generated example. What you will learn The files of a chart and their purpose Chart.yaml: version and appVersion values.yaml as the chart interface _helpers.tpl, NOTES.txt and helm lint Generate and look helm create shop-web tree shop-web shop-web/ Chart.yaml name, version, appVersion, description, dependencies values.yaml default values: the interface of the chart charts/ packaged subcharts (dependencies) templates/ deployment.yaml service.yaml ingress.yaml hpa.yaml serviceaccount.yaml _helpers.tpl named template snippets, not rendered on their own NOTES.txt message printed after install tests/test-connection.yaml .helmignore files to leave out of the package The generated chart is a good starting point but includes things you may not need. Delete what you do not use (the hpa, the test, the ingress) and keep the chart small. Chart.yaml apiVersion: v2 name: shop-web description: The shop web frontend type: application version: 0.3 .1 # version of the chart (packaging) appVersion: \"1.8.2\" # version of the application inside version follows semantic versioning and must increase whenever the chart changes, because repositories and OCI registries treat versions as immutable. appVersion is informational: the version of the app (often the default image tag). Do not confuse them: you may release chart 0.3.2 with the same app, or app 1.9.0 with chart 0.4.0. values.yaml is the interface The values file documents every setting a user may change, with safe defaults and comments: replicaCount: 2 image: repository: registry.example.com/shop/web tag: \"\" # empty means use appVersion pullPolicy: IfNotPresent service: type: ClusterIP port: 80 resources: {} Treat it like an API: keep names stable, group related keys, and avoid exposing every Kubernetes field. Fewer, well named values make a chart easier to use. Add values.schema.json to validate input, so a typo such as replicaCont fails with a message instead of being ignored. Helpers, notes and linting _helpers.tpl defines reusable snippets with define , such as the standard name and labels, used with include . NOTES.txt is a template printed after install; use it for how to reach the app. Check your work often: helm lint shop-web helm template shop-web ./shop-web | kubectl apply --dry-run=client -f - helm package shop-web # creates shop-web-0.3.1.tgz helm lint finds missing fields and bad syntax; rendering and applying with a dry run catches Kubernetes schema errors. Run both in CI for every chart change. Predict first. You change a template but forget to raise Chart.yaml version and push the package to the repository. What goes wrong? Your guess Answer: Users who already have that version will not get the change, or the push is rejected, because chart versions are meant to be immutable. Every published change needs a new chart version. Incident: Same version, different chart Incident Two clusters installed chart 1.2.0 a month apart and behaved differently. $ helm get manifest shop-web -n shop | grep -c \"readinessProbe\" 0 $ helm pull acme/web --version 1.2.0 --untar && grep -c readinessProbe web/templates/deployment.yaml 1 Someone had republished a different package under the same chart version, so the clusters held different content. The repository now refuses to overwrite an existing version, the pipeline bumps the chart version automatically from the change type, and packages are signed or stored in an OCI registry with immutable tags. Common mistakes Reusing the same chart version for changed content Exposing every Kubernetes field as a value Leaving unused generated templates in the chart Skipping helm lint in CI"},"helm/templates-and-the-go-template-language":{"html":"\u003cp>Helm templates are Kubernetes YAML with small pieces of logic inside double curly braces. The logic is Go templating plus a library of helper functions. You do not need all of it. A dozen patterns cover nearly every chart, and the main skill is keeping the output valid YAML, because template whitespace and indentation are where most chart bugs hide.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Insert values and built-in objects\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Use if, with and range\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Define and include helpers, and use toYaml with nindent\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Control whitespace and fail early with required\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"values-and-objects\">Values and objects\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-web\n  labels:\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ .Chart.Name }}\nspec:\n  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: web\n          image: &quot;{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag | default .Chart.AppVersion }}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">apiVersion: apps/v1\nkind: Deployment\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ .Release.Name }}-web\n  labels:\n    app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ .Chart.Name }}\nspec:\n  replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }}\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: web\n          image: &quot;{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag | default .Chart.AppVersion }}&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The top-level objects are \u003ccode>.Values\u003c/code> (your values), \u003ccode>.Release\u003c/code> (name, namespace, revision, whether it is an upgrade), \u003ccode>.Chart\u003c/code> (Chart.yaml fields), and \u003ccode>.Capabilities\u003c/code> (cluster version and API support). The pipe \u003ccode>|\u003c/code> passes a value into a function: \u003ccode>default\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>quote\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>upper\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>trunc\u003c/code>, \u003ccode>sha256sum\u003c/code> and many more. Use \u003ccode>quote\u003c/code> for strings that might look like numbers or booleans.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"conditions-and-loops\">Conditions and loops\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{{- if .Values.ingress.enabled }}\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ .Release.Name }}\nspec:\n  rules:\n    {{- range .Values.ingress.hosts }}\n    - host: {{ . | quote }}\n    {{- end }}\n{{- end }}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">{{- if .Values.ingress.enabled }}\napiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1\nkind: Ingress\nmetadata:\n  name: {{ .Release.Name }}\nspec:\n  rules:\n    {{- range .Values.ingress.hosts }}\n    - host: {{ . | quote }}\n    {{- end }}\n{{- end }}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>if\u003c/code> renders a block only when the value is set (an empty string, zero, false or empty list count as false). \u003ccode>range\u003c/code> repeats a block for each item, with \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> bound to the current item. \u003ccode>with\u003c/code> changes \u003ccode>.\u003c/code> to a value if it is not empty. Inside \u003ccode>range\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>with\u003c/code>, use \u003ccode>$\u003c/code> to reach the root, for example \u003ccode>$.Release.Name\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"helpers-and-toyaml\">Helpers and toYaml\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# templates/_helpers.tpl\n{{- define &quot;web.labels&quot; -}}\napp.kubernetes.io/name: {{ .Chart.Name }}\napp.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ .Release.Name }}\napp.kubernetes.io/version: {{ .Chart.AppVersion | quote }}\n{{- end }}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\"># templates/_helpers.tpl\n{{- define &quot;web.labels&quot; -}}\napp.kubernetes.io/name: {{ .Chart.Name }}\napp.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ .Release.Name }}\napp.kubernetes.io/version: {{ .Chart.AppVersion | quote }}\n{{- end }}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"metadata:\n  labels:\n    {{- include &quot;web.labels&quot; . | nindent 4 }}\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: web\n          resources:\n            {{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 12 }}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">metadata:\n  labels:\n    {{- include &quot;web.labels&quot; . | nindent 4 }}\nspec:\n  template:\n    spec:\n      containers:\n        - name: web\n          resources:\n            {{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 12 }}\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>include\u003c/code> renders a named template and returns a string that you can pipe, unlike \u003ccode>template\u003c/code>. \u003ccode>toYaml\u003c/code> turns a value into YAML text and \u003ccode>nindent N\u003c/code> adds a newline and indents each line by N spaces; the indent must match the position in the file, or the YAML breaks.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"whitespace-and-failing-early\">Whitespace and failing early\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A dash inside braces trims whitespace: \u003ccode>{{-\u003c/code> removes the space before, \u003ccode>-}}\u003c/code> after. Use it so that conditionals do not leave blank lines or break indentation. To stop users from installing without a mandatory value, use \u003ccode>required\u003c/code>:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"image: &quot;{{ required &quot;image.repository is required&quot; .Values.image.repository }}&quot;\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">image: &quot;{{ required &quot;image.repository is required&quot; .Values.image.repository }}&quot;\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>For friendlier errors on bad combinations, call \u003ccode>fail\u003c/code>. Always test with \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> and with several values files, including the case where optional blocks are disabled. Debug with \u003ccode>helm template --debug\u003c/code>, which prints the rendered text even when it is not valid YAML, and use \u003ccode>kubectl apply --dry-run=server\u003c/code> to check against the real API.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A template renders `resources:` followed by lines indented too little, and the install fails with a YAML error. What is the usual cause and fix?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> The nindent number does not match the position of the key. Count the indentation of the parent key and use that plus the child level.\u003c/p>\u003cp>nindent must equal the destination column.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-empty-ingress-that-blocked-a-rollout\">Incident: The empty ingress that blocked a rollout\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A chart upgrade failed in staging only, with a message about an unknown field.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm upgrade shop-web ./shop-web -n shop -f values-staging.yaml\nhelm template ./shop-web -f values-staging.yaml | sed -n 40,50p\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm upgrade shop-web ./shop-web -n shop -f values-staging.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: UPGRADE FAILED: error validating &quot;&quot;: error validating data: unknown field &quot;rules&quot; in io.k8s.api.networking.v1.IngressSpec\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm template ./shop-web -f values-staging.yaml | sed -n 40,50p\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">spec:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  rules:\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>With ingress.hosts empty, the range produced no items, but the template still rendered an empty rules: key with bad indentation. The fix wrapped the whole Ingress in a condition that checks both enabled and a non-empty host list, and the team added a CI job that renders the chart with several values files (all optional features on, all off) and validates the output.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Wrong nindent values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Forgetting quote on strings that look like numbers\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Rendering only with default values in tests\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Putting complex logic in templates\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"values-and-objects","text":"Values and objects"},{"id":"conditions-and-loops","text":"Conditions and loops"},{"id":"helpers-and-toyaml","text":"Helpers and toYaml"},{"id":"whitespace-and-failing-early","text":"Whitespace and failing early"},{"id":"incident-the-empty-ingress-that-blocked-a-rollout","text":"Incident: The empty ingress that blocked a rollout"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm: Chart Template Guide","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/chart_template_guide/"},{"t":"Helm: Template Function List","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/chart_template_guide/function_list/"}],"text":"Helm templates are Kubernetes YAML with small pieces of logic inside double curly braces. The logic is Go templating plus a library of helper functions. You do not need all of it. A dozen patterns cover nearly every chart, and the main skill is keeping the output valid YAML, because template whitespace and indentation are where most chart bugs hide. What you will learn Insert values and built-in objects Use if, with and range Define and include helpers, and use toYaml with nindent Control whitespace and fail early with required Values and objects apiVersion: apps/v1 kind: Deployment metadata: name: {{ .Release.Name }}-web labels: app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ .Chart.Name }} spec: replicas: {{ .Values.replicaCount }} template: spec: containers: - name: web image: \"{{ .Values.image.repository }}:{{ .Values.image.tag | default .Chart.AppVersion }}\" The top-level objects are .Values (your values), .Release (name, namespace, revision, whether it is an upgrade), .Chart (Chart.yaml fields), and .Capabilities (cluster version and API support). The pipe | passes a value into a function: default , quote , upper , trunc , sha256sum and many more. Use quote for strings that might look like numbers or booleans. Conditions and loops {{- if .Values.ingress.enabled }} apiVersion: networking.k8s.io/v1 kind: Ingress metadata: name: {{ .Release.Name }} spec: rules: {{- range .Values.ingress.hosts }} - host: {{ . | quote }} {{- end }} {{- end }} if renders a block only when the value is set (an empty string, zero, false or empty list count as false). range repeats a block for each item, with . bound to the current item. with changes . to a value if it is not empty. Inside range or with , use $ to reach the root, for example $.Release.Name . Helpers and toYaml # templates/_helpers.tpl {{- define \"web.labels\" -}} app.kubernetes.io/name: {{ .Chart.Name }} app.kubernetes.io/instance: {{ .Release.Name }} app.kubernetes.io/version: {{ .Chart.AppVersion | quote }} {{- end }} metadata: labels: {{- include \"web.labels\" . | nindent 4 }} spec: template: spec: containers: - name: web resources: {{- toYaml .Values.resources | nindent 12 }} include renders a named template and returns a string that you can pipe, unlike template . toYaml turns a value into YAML text and nindent N adds a newline and indents each line by N spaces; the indent must match the position in the file, or the YAML breaks. Whitespace and failing early A dash inside braces trims whitespace: {{- removes the space before, -}} after. Use it so that conditionals do not leave blank lines or break indentation. To stop users from installing without a mandatory value, use required : image: \"{{ required \"image.repository is required\" .Values.image.repository }}\" For friendlier errors on bad combinations, call fail . Always test with helm template and with several values files, including the case where optional blocks are disabled. Debug with helm template --debug , which prints the rendered text even when it is not valid YAML, and use kubectl apply --dry-run=server to check against the real API. Predict first. A template renders `resources:` followed by lines indented too little, and the install fails with a YAML error. What is the usual cause and fix? Your guess Answer: The nindent number does not match the position of the key. Count the indentation of the parent key and use that plus the child level. nindent must equal the destination column. Incident: The empty ingress that blocked a rollout Incident A chart upgrade failed in staging only, with a message about an unknown field. $ helm upgrade shop-web ./shop-web -n shop -f values-staging.yaml Error: UPGRADE FAILED: error validating \"\": error validating data: unknown field \"rules\" in io.k8s.api.networking.v1.IngressSpec $ helm template ./shop-web -f values-staging.yaml | sed -n 40,50p spec: rules: With ingress.hosts empty, the range produced no items, but the template still rendered an empty rules: key with bad indentation. The fix wrapped the whole"},"helm/upgrade-rollback-and-release-history":{"html":"\u003cp>Installing is the easy part. The real value of Helm shows when the application changes: each upgrade becomes a numbered revision, and if it goes wrong you can return to a previous one with a single command. Using the right flags turns a failed upgrade into an automatic rollback instead of a half-broken release at 02:00.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Upgrade with upgrade --install and the safety flags\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Read helm history and understand revisions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Roll back and know what a rollback does and does not restore\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Uninstall and keep or remove persistent data\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"upgrade\">Upgrade\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm upgrade --install shop-web acme/web \\\n  --version 2.4.2 \\\n  --namespace shop --create-namespace \\\n  -f values-prod.yaml --set image.tag=1.8.3 \\\n  --atomic --wait --timeout 5m\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm upgrade --install shop-web acme/web \\\n  --version 2.4.2 \\\n  --namespace shop --create-namespace \\\n  -f values-prod.yaml --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">set\u003c/span> image.tag=1.8.3 \\\n  --atomic --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wait\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">timeout\u003c/span> 5m\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>upgrade --install\u003c/code> installs if the release does not exist and upgrades if it does, so one command works for first and later deployments. \u003ccode>--wait\u003c/code> makes Helm wait until Deployments are ready and Jobs have completed, up to the timeout. \u003ccode>--atomic\u003c/code> implies wait and \u003cstrong>rolls back automatically\u003c/strong> if the upgrade fails or times out. Without them, \u003ccode>helm upgrade\u003c/code> returns as soon as the objects are accepted, and a broken rollout is reported as success. Pair them with readiness probes that mean something.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"history\">History\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm history shop-web -n shop\nREVISION  UPDATED                   STATUS      CHART      APP VERSION  DESCRIPTION\n1         Mon Jan  5 09:10:11 2026  superseded  web-2.4.0  1.8.0        Install complete\n2         Mon Jan  5 14:22:40 2026  superseded  web-2.4.1  1.8.2        Upgrade complete\n3         Tue Jan  6 10:05:02 2026  failed      web-2.4.2  1.8.3        Upgrade &quot;shop-web&quot; failed\n4         Tue Jan  6 10:07:30 2026  deployed    web-2.4.1  1.8.2        Rollback to 2\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> shop-web -n shop\nREVISION  UPDATED                   STATUS      CHART      APP VERSION  DESCRIPTION\n1         Mon Jan  5 09:10:11 2026  superseded  web-2.4.0  1.8.0        Install complete\n2         Mon Jan  5 14:22:40 2026  superseded  web-2.4.1  1.8.2        Upgrade complete\n3         Tue Jan  6 10:05:02 2026  failed      web-2.4.2  1.8.3        Upgrade \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;shop-web&quot;\u003c/span> failed\n4         Tue Jan  6 10:07:30 2026  deployed    web-2.4.1  1.8.2        Rollback to 2\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Every change creates a revision, stored as a Secret. Helm keeps ten by default; set \u003ccode>--history-max\u003c/code> to control it. \u003ccode>helm get values --revision 2\u003c/code> and \u003ccode>helm get manifest --revision 2\u003c/code> show exactly what was applied then, which is invaluable for finding what changed.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"rollback\">Rollback\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm rollback shop-web 2 -n shop --wait\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm rollback shop-web 2 -n shop --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wait\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A rollback creates a \u003cstrong>new\u003c/strong> revision (4 in the example) that re-applies the manifest of revision 2. It restores the Kubernetes objects Helm manages. It does \u003cstrong>not\u003c/strong> restore data: a database migration that already ran, a persistent volume that was changed, or a secret edited by hand. Plan upgrades so that the previous version can run against the current data (backward-compatible migrations), and use hooks or a separate step for migrations. Also remember that a rollback can fail if a release is stuck in \u003ccode>pending-upgrade\u003c/code>; the debugging lesson covers that.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"uninstall\">Uninstall\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm uninstall shop-web -n shop --keep-history\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm uninstall shop-web -n shop --keep-history\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Uninstall deletes the objects of the release. PersistentVolumeClaims created by StatefulSets are usually \u003cstrong>kept\u003c/strong> by Kubernetes, and CRDs installed from a chart&#39;s \u003ccode>crds/\u003c/code> directory are not removed by Helm, on purpose. Check what remains with \u003ccode>kubectl get pvc,crd\u003c/code>. Use \u003ccode>--keep-history\u003c/code> if you may want to roll back or inspect the past. In production pipelines, protect critical releases from accidental removal with access controls on who can run uninstall.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> An upgrade changed the database schema and then failed. You run helm rollback. Is the schema restored?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> No. Rollback restores Kubernetes objects, not data or schema. Migrations must be backward compatible or reverted separately.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Helm manages manifests, not your data.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-upgrade-that-said-happy-helming\">Incident: The upgrade that said Happy Helming\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A release reported success while all pods were crash-looping.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml\nkubectl get pods -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Release &quot;shop-web&quot; has been upgraded. Happy Helming!\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>kubectl get pods -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">shop-web-6c9d7b8f4-hq2lm   0/1   CrashLoopBackOff   5   4m\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Without --wait, Helm returned as soon as the manifests were accepted. The pipeline marked the deployment green, and nobody checked the pods. The team added --atomic --wait --timeout 5m to every upgrade, a post-deploy smoke test, and an alert on pods not ready, so a bad release now rolls back by itself within minutes.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Upgrading without --wait or --atomic\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Assuming rollback restores data\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Letting history grow or vanish unexpectedly\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Uninstalling production releases without protection\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"upgrade","text":"Upgrade"},{"id":"history","text":"History"},{"id":"rollback","text":"Rollback"},{"id":"uninstall","text":"Uninstall"},{"id":"incident-the-upgrade-that-said-happy-helming","text":"Incident: The upgrade that said Happy Helming"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"helm upgrade","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/helm/helm_upgrade/"},{"t":"helm rollback","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/helm/helm_rollback/"}],"text":"Installing is the easy part. The real value of Helm shows when the application changes: each upgrade becomes a numbered revision, and if it goes wrong you can return to a previous one with a single command. Using the right flags turns a failed upgrade into an automatic rollback instead of a half-broken release at 02:00. What you will learn Upgrade with upgrade --install and the safety flags Read helm history and understand revisions Roll back and know what a rollback does and does not restore Uninstall and keep or remove persistent data Upgrade helm upgrade --install shop-web acme/web \\ --version 2.4.2 \\ --namespace shop --create-namespace \\ -f values-prod.yaml -- set image.tag=1.8.3 \\ --atomic -- wait -- timeout 5m upgrade --install installs if the release does not exist and upgrades if it does, so one command works for first and later deployments. --wait makes Helm wait until Deployments are ready and Jobs have completed, up to the timeout. --atomic implies wait and rolls back automatically if the upgrade fails or times out. Without them, helm upgrade returns as soon as the objects are accepted, and a broken rollout is reported as success. Pair them with readiness probes that mean something. History helm history shop-web -n shop REVISION UPDATED STATUS CHART APP VERSION DESCRIPTION 1 Mon Jan 5 09:10:11 2026 superseded web-2.4.0 1.8.0 Install complete 2 Mon Jan 5 14:22:40 2026 superseded web-2.4.1 1.8.2 Upgrade complete 3 Tue Jan 6 10:05:02 2026 failed web-2.4.2 1.8.3 Upgrade \"shop-web\" failed 4 Tue Jan 6 10:07:30 2026 deployed web-2.4.1 1.8.2 Rollback to 2 Every change creates a revision, stored as a Secret. Helm keeps ten by default; set --history-max to control it. helm get values --revision 2 and helm get manifest --revision 2 show exactly what was applied then, which is invaluable for finding what changed. Rollback helm rollback shop-web 2 -n shop -- wait A rollback creates a new revision (4 in the example) that re-applies the manifest of revision 2. It restores the Kubernetes objects Helm manages. It does not restore data: a database migration that already ran, a persistent volume that was changed, or a secret edited by hand. Plan upgrades so that the previous version can run against the current data (backward-compatible migrations), and use hooks or a separate step for migrations. Also remember that a rollback can fail if a release is stuck in pending-upgrade ; the debugging lesson covers that. Uninstall helm uninstall shop-web -n shop --keep-history Uninstall deletes the objects of the release. PersistentVolumeClaims created by StatefulSets are usually kept by Kubernetes, and CRDs installed from a chart&#39;s crds/ directory are not removed by Helm, on purpose. Check what remains with kubectl get pvc,crd . Use --keep-history if you may want to roll back or inspect the past. In production pipelines, protect critical releases from accidental removal with access controls on who can run uninstall. Predict first. An upgrade changed the database schema and then failed. You run helm rollback. Is the schema restored? Your guess Answer: No. Rollback restores Kubernetes objects, not data or schema. Migrations must be backward compatible or reverted separately. Helm manages manifests, not your data. Incident: The upgrade that said Happy Helming Incident A release reported success while all pods were crash-looping. $ helm upgrade shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml Release \"shop-web\" has been upgraded. Happy Helming! $ kubectl get pods -n shop shop-web-6c9d7b8f4-hq2lm 0/1 CrashLoopBackOff 5 4m Without --wait, Helm returned as soon as the manifests were accepted. The pipeline marked the deployment green, and nobody checked the pods. The team added --atomic --wait --timeout 5m to every upgrade, a post-deploy smoke test, and an alert on pods not ready, so a bad release now rolls back by itself within minutes. Common mistakes Upgrading without --wait or --atomic Assuming rollback restores data Letting history grow or vanish"},"helm/values-per-environment-and-secrets":{"html":"\u003cp>Once a chart works, the next question is how to configure it for several environments without copy-paste and without leaking a database password into a repository. The answer is a layered set of values files, validation, and a clear rule: values carry configuration, and secrets arrive from somewhere else.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Layer values files: base, environment and one-off\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Validate values with a schema and render in CI\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Why secrets do not belong in values files\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Safe patterns: existing secrets, external secret operators and encrypted files\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"layering\">Layering\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>A repository layout that scales:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"text\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"deploy/shop-web/\n  values.yaml           shared settings for all environments\n  values-dev.yaml       smaller replicas, debug logging\n  values-staging.yaml\n  values-prod.yaml      replicas, resources, hostnames, pod disruption budget\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">deploy/shop-web/\n  values.yaml           shared settings for all environments\n  values-dev.yaml       smaller replicas, debug logging\n  values-staging.yaml\n  values-prod.yaml      replicas, resources, hostnames, pod disruption budget\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm upgrade --install shop-web acme/web -n shop \\\n  -f deploy/shop-web/values.yaml -f deploy/shop-web/values-prod.yaml\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm upgrade --install shop-web acme/web -n shop \\\n  -f deploy/shop-web/values.yaml -f deploy/shop-web/values-prod.yaml\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Later files override earlier ones. Keep the environment files short: only what differs. Review changes to \u003ccode>values-prod.yaml\u003c/code> with the same care as application code, because changing a replica count or a resource limit is a production change.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"validate\">Validate\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Add \u003ccode>values.schema.json\u003c/code> to the chart so wrong types or missing keys fail at install time:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"json\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"{\n  &quot;$schema&quot;: &quot;https://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#&quot;,\n  &quot;type&quot;: &quot;object&quot;,\n  &quot;required&quot;: [&quot;image&quot;],\n  &quot;properties&quot;: {\n    &quot;replicaCount&quot;: { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;integer&quot;, &quot;minimum&quot;: 1 },\n    &quot;image&quot;: {\n      &quot;type&quot;: &quot;object&quot;,\n      &quot;required&quot;: [&quot;repository&quot;],\n      &quot;properties&quot;: { &quot;repository&quot;: { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot; }, &quot;tag&quot;: { &quot;type&quot;: &quot;string&quot; } }\n    }\n  }\n}\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;$schema&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;https://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;object&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;required&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;image&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;properties&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;replicaCount&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;integer&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;minimum&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-number\">1\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;image&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;object&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;required&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">[\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;repository&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">]\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span>\n      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;properties&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;repository&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;string&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">,\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;tag&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">{\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;type&quot;\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;string&quot;\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-punctuation\">}\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>In CI, render every environment (\u003ccode>helm template -f ... | kubeconform\u003c/code> or a policy tool) so a mistake is found in the pull request, not during the deploy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"secrets-and-values\">Secrets and values\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Values are stored in release history (as Secrets, base64 encoded, not encrypted by Helm), appear in \u003ccode>helm get values\u003c/code>, and often sit in a repository. A password in \u003ccode>values-prod.yaml\u003c/code> therefore ends up in git history, in CI logs and in anyone&#39;s terminal who has read access to the release. Do not put secrets in values files or in \u003ccode>--set\u003c/code> arguments that appear in logs.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"better-patterns\">Better patterns\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Reference an existing Secret\u003c/strong>: the chart takes \u003ccode>existingSecret: shop-db\u003c/code> and the Secret is created separately, by an operator or a secure pipeline step.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>External secrets\u003c/strong>: a controller reads from a secret manager and creates the Kubernetes Secret; the chart only names it.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Encrypted files\u003c/strong>: tools such as SOPS keep encrypted values in the repository and decrypt them in the pipeline with a key you control.\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>\u003cstrong>Sealed secrets\u003c/strong>: encrypted objects that only the cluster can decrypt.\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003cp>Whatever you choose, rotate any secret that was ever committed, restrict who can read Secrets in the namespace, and consider enabling encryption at rest for the cluster&#39;s Secret storage. In the chart, mount the secret as an environment variable or file by reference, never by value.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A teammate puts a database password in values-prod.yaml and commits it. Name two places it now exists besides the file.\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> In the repository history, and in the release history stored in the cluster (visible with helm get values). Rotate it.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Values are not a secret store.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-password-in-helm-get-values\">Incident: The password in helm get values\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A contractor with read access to one namespace could see the production database password.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm get values shop-web -n shop\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm get values shop-web -n shop\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">USER-SUPPLIED VALUES:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">db:\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  host: pg.internal\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">  password: Sup3rS3cret!\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The chart took the password as a value, so it was stored in the release Secret and printed by helm get values. The password was rotated, the chart was changed to reference an existing Secret, and an external secrets controller now creates that Secret from the secret manager. The team also tightened RBAC so that reading release Secrets requires an explicit role.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Secrets in values files or --set\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Copy-pasted environment files with unnoticed drift\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>No validation of values\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Giving everyone read access to release Secrets\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"layering","text":"Layering"},{"id":"validate","text":"Validate"},{"id":"secrets-and-values","text":"Secrets and values"},{"id":"better-patterns","text":"Better patterns"},{"id":"incident-the-password-in-helm-get-values","text":"Incident: The password in helm get values"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm: Values","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/chart_best_practices/values/"},{"t":"Kubernetes: Secrets","u":"https://kubernetes.io/docs/concepts/configuration/secret/"}],"text":"Once a chart works, the next question is how to configure it for several environments without copy-paste and without leaking a database password into a repository. The answer is a layered set of values files, validation, and a clear rule: values carry configuration, and secrets arrive from somewhere else. What you will learn Layer values files: base, environment and one-off Validate values with a schema and render in CI Why secrets do not belong in values files Safe patterns: existing secrets, external secret operators and encrypted files Layering A repository layout that scales: deploy/shop-web/ values.yaml shared settings for all environments values-dev.yaml smaller replicas, debug logging values-staging.yaml values-prod.yaml replicas, resources, hostnames, pod disruption budget helm upgrade --install shop-web acme/web -n shop \\ -f deploy/shop-web/values.yaml -f deploy/shop-web/values-prod.yaml Later files override earlier ones. Keep the environment files short: only what differs. Review changes to values-prod.yaml with the same care as application code, because changing a replica count or a resource limit is a production change. Validate Add values.schema.json to the chart so wrong types or missing keys fail at install time: { \"$schema\" : \"https://json-schema.org/draft-07/schema#\" , \"type\" : \"object\" , \"required\" : [ \"image\" ] , \"properties\" : { \"replicaCount\" : { \"type\" : \"integer\" , \"minimum\" : 1 } , \"image\" : { \"type\" : \"object\" , \"required\" : [ \"repository\" ] , \"properties\" : { \"repository\" : { \"type\" : \"string\" } , \"tag\" : { \"type\" : \"string\" } } } } } In CI, render every environment ( helm template -f ... | kubeconform or a policy tool) so a mistake is found in the pull request, not during the deploy. Secrets and values Values are stored in release history (as Secrets, base64 encoded, not encrypted by Helm), appear in helm get values , and often sit in a repository. A password in values-prod.yaml therefore ends up in git history, in CI logs and in anyone&#39;s terminal who has read access to the release. Do not put secrets in values files or in --set arguments that appear in logs. Better patterns Reference an existing Secret : the chart takes existingSecret: shop-db and the Secret is created separately, by an operator or a secure pipeline step. External secrets : a controller reads from a secret manager and creates the Kubernetes Secret; the chart only names it. Encrypted files : tools such as SOPS keep encrypted values in the repository and decrypt them in the pipeline with a key you control. Sealed secrets : encrypted objects that only the cluster can decrypt. Whatever you choose, rotate any secret that was ever committed, restrict who can read Secrets in the namespace, and consider enabling encryption at rest for the cluster&#39;s Secret storage. In the chart, mount the secret as an environment variable or file by reference, never by value. Predict first. A teammate puts a database password in values-prod.yaml and commits it. Name two places it now exists besides the file. Your guess Answer: In the repository history, and in the release history stored in the cluster (visible with helm get values). Rotate it. Values are not a secret store. Incident: The password in helm get values Incident A contractor with read access to one namespace could see the production database password. $ helm get values shop-web -n shop USER-SUPPLIED VALUES: db: host: pg.internal password: Sup3rS3cret! The chart took the password as a value, so it was stored in the release Secret and printed by helm get values. The password was rotated, the chart was changed to reference an existing Secret, and an external secrets controller now creates that Secret from the secret manager. The team also tightened RBAC so that reading release Secrets requires an explicit role. Common mistakes Secrets in values files or --set Copy-pasted environment files with unnoticed drift No validation of values Giving everyone read access to release Secrets"},"helm/dependencies-subcharts-and-registries":{"html":"\u003cp>Your application needs a database or a cache, and someone has already published a chart for it. Helm lets a chart declare dependencies, so one install can bring up the whole stack. It also lets you publish your own charts to the same registry where you keep container images. Dependencies save time and add risk, so it matters how you pin, review and update them.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Declare dependencies and update them\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Pass values to subcharts and use conditions\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Package and push a chart to an OCI registry\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Version, pin and update dependencies safely\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"declare-dependencies\">Declare dependencies\u003c/h2>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"# Chart.yaml\ndependencies:\n  - name: redis\n    version: &quot;19.6.2&quot;\n    repository: https://charts.example.com\n    condition: redis.enabled\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># Chart.yaml\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">dependencies:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-bullet\">-\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">name:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">redis\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">version:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;19.6.2&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">repository:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">https://charts.example.com\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">condition:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">redis.enabled\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm dependency update shop-web      # downloads into charts/ and writes Chart.lock\nhelm dependency build shop-web       # rebuild from Chart.lock (use in CI)\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm dependency update shop-web      \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># downloads into charts/ and writes Chart.lock\u003c/span>\nhelm dependency build shop-web       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># rebuild from Chart.lock (use in CI)\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>\u003ccode>Chart.lock\u003c/code> records exact versions and digests, like a lockfile. Commit it. In CI use \u003ccode>helm dependency build\u003c/code> so the build uses exactly what was reviewed. The dependency becomes a \u003cstrong>subchart\u003c/strong> installed together with your chart in the same release.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"passing-values\">Passing values\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Values for a subchart go under its name:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"redis:\n  enabled: true\n  auth:\n    existingSecret: shop-redis\n  architecture: standalone\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">redis:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">enabled:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-literal\">true\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">auth:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">existingSecret:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">shop-redis\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">architecture:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">standalone\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The \u003ccode>condition\u003c/code> in \u003ccode>Chart.yaml\u003c/code> lets users turn the subchart off, for example when production uses a managed cache. A subchart cannot read the parent&#39;s values, but a parent can pass them down, and \u003ccode>global:\u003c/code> values are visible to all subcharts. Remember that a subchart&#39;s own defaults may not be production safe (persistence off, no replicas, no auth); read them like any chart.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"oci-registries\">OCI registries\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Helm can store charts in the same kind of registry as container images:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm registry login registry.example.com\nhelm package shop-web                       # shop-web-0.3.1.tgz\nhelm push shop-web-0.3.1.tgz oci://registry.example.com/charts\nhelm install shop-web oci://registry.example.com/charts/shop-web --version 0.3.1 -n shop\nhelm pull oci://registry.example.com/charts/shop-web --version 0.3.1\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm registry login registry.example.com\nhelm package shop-web                       \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># shop-web-0.3.1.tgz\u003c/span>\nhelm push shop-web-0.3.1.tgz oci://registry.example.com/charts\nhelm install shop-web oci://registry.example.com/charts/shop-web --version 0.3.1 -n shop\nhelm pull oci://registry.example.com/charts/shop-web --version 0.3.1\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>OCI gives you the registry&#39;s access control, immutability settings and signing tools, and one place for images and charts. Classic HTTP repositories with an \u003ccode>index.yaml\u003c/code> still work and are served by tools such as ChartMuseum or static hosting. Choose one and document it.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"versions-and-updates\">Versions and updates\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Chart \u003ccode>version\u003c/code> is SemVer: raise MAJOR for breaking value changes, MINOR for new features, PATCH for fixes. Pin dependency versions exactly, or with a narrow range, and update on purpose: open a pull request that bumps the version, shows \u003ccode>helm template\u003c/code> differences, and runs your tests. Automated tools can propose these updates. Subscribe to release notes of important charts, watch for deprecations of Kubernetes APIs, and keep an internal mirror of external charts you depend on, so a deleted upstream does not stop deployments.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> Your chart depends on `redis` at &quot;^19.0.0&quot; and a new minor release changes defaults. What can happen and how do you avoid it?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> A new install may silently get different defaults. Pin an exact version, commit Chart.lock, and update through a reviewed pull request.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Locks make builds repeatable.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-the-dependency-that-disappeared\">Incident: The dependency that disappeared\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A deployment pipeline failed on a Monday morning with a missing chart error.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm dependency update shop-web\ncurl -sI https://charts.old-example.com/index.yaml | head -1\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm dependency update shop-web\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: can't get a valid version for repositories redis. Try changing the version constraint in Chart.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>curl -sI https://charts.old-example.com/index.yaml | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">head\u003c/span> -1\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">HTTP/2 404\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The upstream repository had been moved and the old URL returned 404, so every build failed. The team mirrored all external charts into their own registry, switched dependencies to the mirror, and used Chart.lock with digests. A weekly job checks the upstream for new versions and opens a pull request.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Floating dependency ranges\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Not committing Chart.lock\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Trusting subchart defaults for production\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Depending on third-party repositories without a mirror\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"declare-dependencies","text":"Declare dependencies"},{"id":"passing-values","text":"Passing values"},{"id":"oci-registries","text":"OCI registries"},{"id":"versions-and-updates","text":"Versions and updates"},{"id":"incident-the-dependency-that-disappeared","text":"Incident: The dependency that disappeared"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm: Chart dependencies","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/helm/helm_dependency/"},{"t":"Helm: Registries (OCI)","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/topics/registries/"}],"text":"Your application needs a database or a cache, and someone has already published a chart for it. Helm lets a chart declare dependencies, so one install can bring up the whole stack. It also lets you publish your own charts to the same registry where you keep container images. Dependencies save time and add risk, so it matters how you pin, review and update them. What you will learn Declare dependencies and update them Pass values to subcharts and use conditions Package and push a chart to an OCI registry Version, pin and update dependencies safely Declare dependencies # Chart.yaml dependencies: - name: redis version: \"19.6.2\" repository: https://charts.example.com condition: redis.enabled helm dependency update shop-web # downloads into charts/ and writes Chart.lock helm dependency build shop-web # rebuild from Chart.lock (use in CI) Chart.lock records exact versions and digests, like a lockfile. Commit it. In CI use helm dependency build so the build uses exactly what was reviewed. The dependency becomes a subchart installed together with your chart in the same release. Passing values Values for a subchart go under its name: redis: enabled: true auth: existingSecret: shop-redis architecture: standalone The condition in Chart.yaml lets users turn the subchart off, for example when production uses a managed cache. A subchart cannot read the parent&#39;s values, but a parent can pass them down, and global: values are visible to all subcharts. Remember that a subchart&#39;s own defaults may not be production safe (persistence off, no replicas, no auth); read them like any chart. OCI registries Helm can store charts in the same kind of registry as container images: helm registry login registry.example.com helm package shop-web # shop-web-0.3.1.tgz helm push shop-web-0.3.1.tgz oci://registry.example.com/charts helm install shop-web oci://registry.example.com/charts/shop-web --version 0.3.1 -n shop helm pull oci://registry.example.com/charts/shop-web --version 0.3.1 OCI gives you the registry&#39;s access control, immutability settings and signing tools, and one place for images and charts. Classic HTTP repositories with an index.yaml still work and are served by tools such as ChartMuseum or static hosting. Choose one and document it. Versions and updates Chart version is SemVer: raise MAJOR for breaking value changes, MINOR for new features, PATCH for fixes. Pin dependency versions exactly, or with a narrow range, and update on purpose: open a pull request that bumps the version, shows helm template differences, and runs your tests. Automated tools can propose these updates. Subscribe to release notes of important charts, watch for deprecations of Kubernetes APIs, and keep an internal mirror of external charts you depend on, so a deleted upstream does not stop deployments. Predict first. Your chart depends on `redis` at \"^19.0.0\" and a new minor release changes defaults. What can happen and how do you avoid it? Your guess Answer: A new install may silently get different defaults. Pin an exact version, commit Chart.lock, and update through a reviewed pull request. Locks make builds repeatable. Incident: The dependency that disappeared Incident A deployment pipeline failed on a Monday morning with a missing chart error. $ helm dependency update shop-web Error: can't get a valid version for repositories redis. Try changing the version constraint in Chart.yaml $ curl -sI https://charts.old-example.com/index.yaml | head -1 HTTP/2 404 The upstream repository had been moved and the old URL returned 404, so every build failed. The team mirrored all external charts into their own registry, switched dependencies to the mirror, and used Chart.lock with digests. A weekly job checks the upstream for new versions and opens a pull request. Common mistakes Floating dependency ranges Not committing Chart.lock Trusting subchart defaults for production Depending on third-party repositories without a mirror"},"helm/debugging-helm-releases":{"html":"\u003cp>Helm failures come in a few families: the template does not render, the manifests render but Kubernetes rejects them, the manifests apply but the pods never become ready, or the release itself is stuck in a bad state. Each has a distinct signature and a distinct tool. A short checklist saves a lot of time and avoids the worst reflex, which is deleting things until it works.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"what-you-will-learn\">What you will learn\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Separate render errors from API errors and rollout errors\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Fix a release stuck in pending-upgrade\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Understand hooks and use helm test\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Detect drift and compare with helm diff\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n\u003ch2 id=\"where-did-it-fail\">Where did it fail?\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Work from the left of this pipeline: render, validate, apply, roll out.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm lint ./chart -f values.yaml\nhelm template rel ./chart -f values.yaml --debug | less     # render problems\nhelm template rel ./chart -f values.yaml | kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f -   # API problems\nhelm upgrade rel ./chart -f values.yaml --wait --timeout 5m --debug\nkubectl get events -n NS --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail\nkubectl rollout status deploy/NAME -n NS\nkubectl describe pod POD -n NS\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm lint ./chart -f values.yaml\nhelm template rel ./chart -f values.yaml --debug | less     \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># render problems\u003c/span>\nhelm template rel ./chart -f values.yaml | kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f -   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># API problems\u003c/span>\nhelm upgrade rel ./chart -f values.yaml --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">wait\u003c/span> --\u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">timeout\u003c/span> 5m --debug\nkubectl get events -n NS --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span>\nkubectl rollout status deploy/NAME -n NS\nkubectl describe pod POD -n NS\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>A message like \u003ccode>YAML parse error\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>nil pointer evaluating interface\u003c/code> is a \u003cstrong>render\u003c/strong> problem (look at the template and values). \u003ccode>unknown field\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>invalid value\u003c/code> is an \u003cstrong>API\u003c/strong> problem (the manifest is wrong for the cluster version). \u003ccode>timed out waiting for the condition\u003c/code> is a \u003cstrong>rollout\u003c/strong> problem: the objects were accepted but pods are not ready, so debug them with kubectl as in the Kubernetes debugging lesson.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"stuck-releases\">Stuck releases\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>If an upgrade was interrupted (a CI job was killed), the release can stay in \u003ccode>pending-upgrade\u003c/code> or \u003ccode>pending-install\u003c/code>, and the next command says \u003ccode>another operation (install/upgrade/rollback) is in progress\u003c/code>.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm history rel -n NS            # find the stuck revision and the last deployed one\nhelm rollback rel 7 -n NS         # roll back to the last good revision (often fixes it)\n# last resort: remove the stuck revision record, never the workload\nkubectl delete secret sh.helm.release.v1.rel.v9 -n NS\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> rel -n NS            \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># find the stuck revision and the last deployed one\u003c/span>\nhelm rollback rel 7 -n NS         \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># roll back to the last good revision (often fixes it)\u003c/span>\n\u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># last resort: remove the stuck revision record, never the workload\u003c/span>\nkubectl delete secret sh.helm.release.v1.rel.v9 -n NS\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Deleting the release Secret of the stuck revision removes only Helm&#39;s record of it. Do this only after checking the history, and prefer a rollback. Also look for \u003cstrong>ownership errors\u003c/strong>, such as \u003ccode>invalid ownership metadata\u003c/code>, which mean an object already exists and was created outside this release: adopt it with the Helm labels and annotations or remove the duplicate.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"hooks-and-tests\">Hooks and tests\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Hooks run Jobs or Pods at points in the lifecycle, marked by annotations:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"yaml\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"metadata:\n  annotations:\n    &quot;helm.sh/hook&quot;: pre-upgrade\n    &quot;helm.sh/hook-weight&quot;: &quot;0&quot;\n    &quot;helm.sh/hook-delete-policy&quot;: before-hook-creation,hook-succeeded\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">metadata:\u003c/span>\n  \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">annotations:\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;helm.sh/hook&quot;:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">pre-upgrade\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;helm.sh/hook-weight&quot;:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">&quot;0&quot;\u003c/span>\n    \u003cspan class=\"hljs-attr\">&quot;helm.sh/hook-delete-policy&quot;:\u003c/span> \u003cspan class=\"hljs-string\">before-hook-creation,hook-succeeded\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Common uses are database migrations (\u003ccode>pre-upgrade\u003c/code>) and cleanup. Hooks are not part of the release&#39;s tracked objects, so they are not removed on uninstall unless the delete policy says so. A hook that fails fails the whole upgrade: check its Job logs. Chart tests are Pods with \u003ccode>helm.sh/hook: test\u003c/code>; run them with \u003ccode>helm test rel -n NS\u003c/code> as a smoke test after each deploy.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"drift-and-diff\">Drift and diff\u003c/h2>\n\u003cp>Someone edited a Deployment with kubectl, and the next upgrade surprises everyone. Compare before you apply:\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code\" data-lang=\"bash\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm plugin install PLUGIN_URL   # install once; see the plugin project page\nhelm diff upgrade rel ./chart -f values.yaml -n NS\nhelm get manifest rel -n NS | kubectl diff -f -\" aria-label=\"Copy code\">Copy\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">helm plugin install PLUGIN_URL   \u003cspan class=\"hljs-comment\"># install once; see the plugin project page\u003c/span>\nhelm diff upgrade rel ./chart -f values.yaml -n NS\nhelm get manifest rel -n NS | kubectl diff -f -\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>The diff plugin shows what an upgrade would change in the cluster compared with the stored release. Add it to the pipeline, and consider an operator or controller that continuously applies the chart from version control so the cluster state is always reconciled to what was reviewed. Keep \u003ccode>kubectl edit\u003c/code> for investigations, not for production changes.\u003c/p>\n\u003cdiv class=\"predict\">\u003cp class=\"predict-q\">\u003cstrong>Predict first.\u003c/strong> A release is stuck in pending-upgrade after a CI job was killed. What do you try first, and what is the last resort?\u003c/p>\u003clabel class=\"predict-l\">Your guess\u003ctextarea rows=\"2\" class=\"predict-guess\" placeholder=\"Type what you expect, then reveal the answer.\">\u003c/textarea>\u003c/label>\u003cbutton type=\"button\" class=\"btn btn-primary\" data-act=\"predict-reveal\" aria-expanded=\"false\">Reveal the answer\u003c/button>\u003cdiv class=\"predict-out\" hidden>\u003cp class=\"predict-ans\">\u003cstrong>Answer:\u003c/strong> First check helm history and roll back to the last deployed revision. As a last resort delete the stuck revision's release Secret, which removes Helm's record only.\u003c/p>\u003cp>Prefer rollback; do not delete the workload.\u003c/p>\u003c/div>\u003c/div>\n\u003ch2 id=\"incident-another-operation-is-in-progress\">Incident: Another operation is in progress\u003c/h2>\n\u003caside class=\"callout callout-incident\">\u003cdiv class=\"callout-title\">Incident\u003c/div>\u003cp>A pipeline failed on every run after a runner was terminated in the middle of a deploy.\u003c/p>\n\u003c/aside>\n\u003cdiv class=\"code console\" data-lang=\"terminal\">\u003cbutton class=\"copy\" type=\"button\" data-copy=\"helm upgrade --install shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml\nhelm history shop-web -n shop | tail -3\" aria-label=\"Copy commands\">Copy commands\u003c/button>\u003cpre tabindex=\"0\">\u003ccode class=\"hljs\">\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm upgrade --install shop-web acme/web -n shop -f values-prod.yaml\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">Error: UPGRADE FAILED: another operation (install/upgrade/rollback) is in progress\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln cmd\">\u003cspan class=\"ps\" aria-hidden=\"true\">$ \u003c/span>helm \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">history\u003c/span> shop-web -n shop | \u003cspan class=\"hljs-built_in\">tail\u003c/span> -3\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">8   Mon Jan 5 10:10:01 2026  deployed         web-2.4.1  1.8.2  Upgrade complete\u003c/span>\u003cspan class=\"ln out\">9   Mon Jan 5 10:21:33 2026  pending-upgrade  web-2.4.2  1.8.3  Preparing upgrade\u003c/span>\u003c/code>\u003c/pre>\u003c/div>\n\u003cp>Revision 9 had been left pending when the runner died. A rollback to revision 8 reset the state, and the next upgrade succeeded. The team then set a pipeline timeout that is longer than helm&#39;s own timeout, so the job is not killed mid-flight, added --atomic, and wrote this procedure in the runbook so on-call does not delete anything it does not understand.\u003c/p>\n\u003ch2 id=\"common-mistakes\">Common mistakes\u003c/h2>\n\u003cul>\n\u003cli>Deleting resources by hand to make an upgrade pass\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Killing pipeline jobs during a helm operation\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Ignoring failed hooks\u003c/li>\n\u003cli>Making manual edits in the cluster\u003c/li>\n\u003c/ul>\n","toc":[{"id":"what-you-will-learn","text":"What you will learn"},{"id":"where-did-it-fail","text":"Where did it fail?"},{"id":"stuck-releases","text":"Stuck releases"},{"id":"hooks-and-tests","text":"Hooks and tests"},{"id":"drift-and-diff","text":"Drift and diff"},{"id":"incident-another-operation-is-in-progress","text":"Incident: Another operation is in progress"},{"id":"common-mistakes","text":"Common mistakes"}],"refs":[{"t":"Helm: Chart Hooks","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/topics/charts_hooks/"},{"t":"Helm: Debugging templates","u":"https://helm.sh/docs/chart_template_guide/debugging/"}],"text":"Helm failures come in a few families: the template does not render, the manifests render but Kubernetes rejects them, the manifests apply but the pods never become ready, or the release itself is stuck in a bad state. Each has a distinct signature and a distinct tool. A short checklist saves a lot of time and avoids the worst reflex, which is deleting things until it works. What you will learn Separate render errors from API errors and rollout errors Fix a release stuck in pending-upgrade Understand hooks and use helm test Detect drift and compare with helm diff Where did it fail? Work from the left of this pipeline: render, validate, apply, roll out. helm lint ./chart -f values.yaml helm template rel ./chart -f values.yaml --debug | less # render problems helm template rel ./chart -f values.yaml | kubectl apply --dry-run=server -f - # API problems helm upgrade rel ./chart -f values.yaml -- wait -- timeout 5m --debug kubectl get events -n NS --sort-by=.lastTimestamp | tail kubectl rollout status deploy/NAME -n NS kubectl describe pod POD -n NS A message like YAML parse error or nil pointer evaluating interface is a render problem (look at the template and values). unknown field or invalid value is an API problem (the manifest is wrong for the cluster version). timed out waiting for the condition is a rollout problem: the objects were accepted but pods are not ready, so debug them with kubectl as in the Kubernetes debugging lesson. Stuck releases If an upgrade was interrupted (a CI job was killed), the release can stay in pending-upgrade or pending-install , and the next command says another operation (install/upgrade/rollback) is in progress . helm history rel -n NS # find the stuck revision and the last deployed one helm rollback rel 7 -n NS # roll back to the last good revision (often fixes it) # last resort: remove the stuck revision record, never the workload kubectl delete secret sh.helm.release.v1.rel.v9 -n NS Deleting the release Secret of the stuck revision removes only Helm&#39;s record of it. Do this only after checking the history, and prefer a rollback. Also look for ownership errors , such as invalid ownership metadata , which mean an object already exists and was created outside this release: adopt it with the Helm labels and annotations or remove the duplicate. Hooks and tests Hooks run Jobs or Pods at points in the lifecycle, marked by annotations: metadata: annotations: \"helm.sh/hook\": pre-upgrade \"helm.sh/hook-weight\": \"0\" \"helm.sh/hook-delete-policy\": before-hook-creation,hook-succeeded Common uses are database migrations ( pre-upgrade ) and cleanup. Hooks are not part of the release&#39;s tracked objects, so they are not removed on uninstall unless the delete policy says so. A hook that fails fails the whole upgrade: check its Job logs. Chart tests are Pods with helm.sh/hook: test ; run them with helm test rel -n NS as a smoke test after each deploy. Drift and diff Someone edited a Deployment with kubectl, and the next upgrade surprises everyone. Compare before you apply: helm plugin install PLUGIN_URL # install once; see the plugin project page helm diff upgrade rel ./chart -f values.yaml -n NS helm get manifest rel -n NS | kubectl diff -f - The diff plugin shows what an upgrade would change in the cluster compared with the stored release. Add it to the pipeline, and consider an operator or controller that continuously applies the chart from version control so the cluster state is always reconciled to what was reviewed. Keep kubectl edit for investigations, not for production changes. Predict first. A release is stuck in pending-upgrade after a CI job was killed. What do you try first, and what is the last resort? Your guess Answer: First check helm history and roll back to the last deployed revision. As a last resort delete the stuck revision's release Secret, which removes Helm's record only. Prefer rollback; do not delete the workload. Incident: Another operation is in progress Incident A pipeline faile"}});